WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:05.390
Hollywood had its biggest year ever in 2018
- despite streaming sites like Netflix and

00:00:05.390 --> 00:00:07.160
Hulu staking a claim on its territory.

00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:11.660
But thanks to this year's crop of amazing
films, that box office record might not last

00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:12.200
for long.

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:15.540
Here are the best movies of 2019... so far.

00:00:16.540 --> 00:00:20.660
If there were any concerns that writer/director
Jordan Peele's Academy Award-winning debut

00:00:20.660 --> 00:00:24.140
Get Out was a fluke, they were quickly laid
to rest by Us.

00:00:24.570 --> 00:00:29.080
In the film, a family is taunted by evil versions
of themselves… and it's highly unsettling

00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:29.580
stuff.

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:31.980
"Who are you people?"

00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:35.360
"It's us."

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:38.760
Critics were particularly stunned by Lupita
Nyong'o's performance.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:40.380
The Lamplight Review noted that:

00:00:40.770 --> 00:00:45.760
"Performances in genre pieces are often dismissed
and disregarded, but make no mistake: Nyong'o's

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:48.300
work in Us is some of the very best of her
career."

00:00:48.730 --> 00:00:53.000
Critics were also impressed by Peele's deft
mixing of comedic elements with nerve-jangling

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:53.500
terror.

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:57.360
A review in Splice Today succinctly summed
up his achievement, claiming that

00:00:57.580 --> 00:01:02.240
"Us combines masterful visuals with dense
storytelling, and a half-dozen instant-classic

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:02.860
sequences.

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:04.320
And it's scary as hell."

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:09.180
Right now, writer/director Nia DaCosta only
has a handful of credits to her name.

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:13.700
But thanks to Little Woods, her insanely self-assured
debut feature, that's about to change in a

00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:14.200
hurry.

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:18.480
DaCosta wrote the screenplay for Little Woods;
a story about two troubled sisters.

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:22.340
One of them is a convicted drug runner who's
trying to get through her last few days of

00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:22.920
probation.

00:01:23.350 --> 00:01:27.300
Unfortunately, she's forced to return to the
drug trade after her mother dies, leaving

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:29.360
behind a hefty mortgage on the family home.

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:31.240
"I know you're selling again.

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:36.480
And I don't appreciate losing customers because
you decided to get back in the business."

00:01:36.740 --> 00:01:41.200
By casting Tessa Thompson in the lead role
of Ollie, DaCosta nabbed one of the hottest

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:42.420
actresses working today.

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:46.459
Her performance was roundly praised, with
descriptors like "towering" and "riveting"

00:01:46.460 --> 00:01:47.640
being par for the course.

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:52.420
But it's DaCosta's keen eye for small details
and sweeping, cinematic shots that really

00:01:52.420 --> 00:01:53.980
had the critics singing her praises.

00:01:54.140 --> 00:01:55.780
And she's just getting started, too.

00:01:56.180 --> 00:02:00.520
In fact, she's all set to update the horror
classic Candyman for producer Jordan Peele.

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:02.880
And that should definitely be a sight to see.

00:02:03.800 --> 00:02:07.420
There's been more written about Avengers:
Endgame than any other cinematic release in

00:02:07.420 --> 00:02:08.140
recent memory.

00:02:08.569 --> 00:02:12.220
But the fact that it's really good film is
often buried beneath the reams of press and

00:02:12.220 --> 00:02:12.720
publicity.

00:02:13.220 --> 00:02:17.799
Avengers: Endgame is brimming with jaw-dropping
action, a surprising amount of humor, and

00:02:17.799 --> 00:02:22.159
the kind of emotional resonance that's sure
to captivate diehard fans and casual moviegoers

00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:22.660
alike.

00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:24.160
"You trust me?"

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:26.320
"I do."

00:02:26.620 --> 00:02:30.640
Many critics pegged Avengers: Endgame as one
of the greatest acts of fan service ever committed

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:31.220
to film.

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:35.650
And while familiarity with the MCU is pretty
much required to make it a complete viewing

00:02:35.650 --> 00:02:39.620
experience, the movie is even more impressive
for how well it works on its own terms.

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:41.480
Flick Filosopher enthused that

00:02:41.900 --> 00:02:45.060
"[Avengers: Endgame is] a miraculous blend
of grief and humor.

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:47.780
Big, bold, brash, then sneakily meta."

00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:52.660
Siftpop stated in no uncertain terms just
how much the film rocks, writing that:

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:56.800
"The word epic gets thrown around so much
that we've forgotten what it actually means.

00:02:56.960 --> 00:02:58.680
This movie is here to remind you."

00:02:58.780 --> 00:03:02.799
Meanwhile, the London Evening Standard apparently
couldn't say enough good things about the

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:03.799
film, writing that:

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:08.060
"The only complaint about Avengers: Endgame
is that it raises the bar so high that there

00:03:08.060 --> 00:03:10.780
may well never be a superhero movie to match
it."

00:03:11.830 --> 00:03:15.989
The John Wick series has never aspired to
be high art… but it just might be the purest

00:03:15.989 --> 00:03:18.720
adrenaline delivery device in the history
of modern cinema.

00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:23.380
Three films in, director Chad Stahelski and
star Keanu Reeves have really perfected the

00:03:23.380 --> 00:03:23.880
formula.

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:28.920
John Wick: Chapter 3 - Parabellum gives diehard
action fans exactly what they came for - and

00:03:28.920 --> 00:03:29.420
then some.

00:03:29.800 --> 00:03:33.660
It's easy to see why Reeves' beleaguered hitman
is swiftly becoming an icon.

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:38.000
Let it suffice to say, the actor's committed
performance and Stahleski's sharp direction

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:40.580
had critics applauding the third entry in
the series.

00:03:40.840 --> 00:03:42.200
Scotland's The Herald wrote:

00:03:42.680 --> 00:03:44.540
"[Reeves] has charisma to burn.

00:03:44.540 --> 00:03:48.800
By the time two hours are up, Wick is once
again living up to the Latin in the title,

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:50.920
desiring peace but preparing for war.

00:03:51.170 --> 00:03:52.540
Fetch, John, Fetch."

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:55.500
In its own highly positive review, The Movie
Blog said

00:03:55.880 --> 00:03:59.300
"John Wick 3 is a buffet of action that only
leaves you wanting more."

00:03:59.720 --> 00:04:02.480
Flickering Myth was just as enthusiastic,
writing that:

00:04:02.620 --> 00:04:07.219
"Where most franchises begin to stumble around
movie three, John Wick: Chapter 3 is the best

00:04:07.220 --> 00:04:09.080
in this highly entertaining series."

00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:14.500
The rom-com genre isn't exactly known for
its inventive and intriguing storylines.

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:18.560
But thanks to a whip-smart screenplay, Long
Shot is something truly special.

00:04:18.940 --> 00:04:23.430
Seth Rogen stars as Fred Flarsky, a journalist
who ends up reconnecting with his childhood

00:04:23.430 --> 00:04:26.340
babysitter Charlotte Field, played by Charlize
Theron.

00:04:26.760 --> 00:04:29.980
It turns out Charlotte's become a highly powerful
political figure.

00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:32.380
In fact, she's gearing up for a presidential
run.

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:36.970
When Charlotte impulsively appoints Fred to
be her speechwriter, events take a decidedly

00:04:36.970 --> 00:04:37.940
romantic turn.

00:04:38.420 --> 00:04:42.220
The chemistry between the two leads is really
something to marvel at, as you can see for

00:04:42.220 --> 00:04:42.720
yourself:

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:43.860
"You asked."

00:04:43.860 --> 00:04:47.240
"No, hey, I've hooked up with my fair share
of world leaders too."

00:04:47.500 --> 00:04:50.460
Critics absolutely loved the film, with the
Daily Herald gushing:

00:04:50.840 --> 00:04:54.460
"What are the odds that this zany rom-com
would turn out to be the funniest crowd-pleaser

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:55.980
of 2019 so far?"

00:04:56.520 --> 00:04:59.360
The Mail on Sunday was equally effusive in
its praise:

00:04:59.660 --> 00:05:03.880
"This is surely one of the most welcome surprises
of the cinematic year, with Jonathan Levine's

00:05:03.880 --> 00:05:08.699
enjoyable film showing that unorthodox casting
combinations can work and that there is still

00:05:08.700 --> 00:05:10.060
life in the romantic comedy.

00:05:10.440 --> 00:05:10.940
Hurrah!"

00:05:11.880 --> 00:05:15.800
An updated, family-friendly take on the King
Arthur legend, The Kid Who Would Be King had

00:05:15.800 --> 00:05:18.040
a lot of things going for it right out of
the gate.

00:05:18.440 --> 00:05:22.700
It's the second directorial effort from Joe
Cornish, who helmed the excellent sci-fi comedy

00:05:22.700 --> 00:05:26.320
Attack the Block and co-wrote the original
script for Marvel's Ant-Man.

00:05:26.710 --> 00:05:31.380
The film's casting is pure genius, with Sir
Patrick Stewart as the wizard Merlin and Rebecca

00:05:31.380 --> 00:05:33.540
Ferguson as the evil enchantress Morgana.

00:05:34.260 --> 00:05:39.310
Louis Ashborne Serkis stars as a British schoolboy
who stumbles upon the mythical sword Excalibur

00:05:39.310 --> 00:05:41.420
and must embrace his identity as a leader.

00:05:41.820 --> 00:05:46.140
Unfortunately, The Kid Who Would Be King was
a commercial failure for Fox - but it certainly

00:05:46.140 --> 00:05:47.340
scored big with critics.

00:05:47.700 --> 00:05:48.460
The Guardian said,

00:05:48.840 --> 00:05:52.580
"[The film is] sentimental in all the right
places, and impossible to dislike."

00:05:52.960 --> 00:05:56.600
Meanwhile, One Room With A View praised Cornish's
direction, claiming that

00:05:56.980 --> 00:06:00.759
"[He delivers a] sword-and-sorcery adventure
with the intelligence and sensitivity that

00:06:00.760 --> 00:06:03.580
kids' films deserve, but do not always receive."

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:08.600
Hopefully, the film's poor box-office performance
won't diminish Cornish's future prospects.

00:06:08.740 --> 00:06:12.520
He's got talent to spare, and he's proven
to be highly skilled at juggling comedic and

00:06:12.540 --> 00:06:15.620
dramatic elements in the service of his wholly
unique visions.

00:06:16.580 --> 00:06:20.960
In order to succeed, Captain Marvel had to
give us a compelling origin story and set

00:06:20.960 --> 00:06:22.380
the scene for Avengers: Endgame.

00:06:22.880 --> 00:06:26.720
Indie filmmakers Anna Boden and Ryan Fleck
rose to the occasion brilliantly, and their

00:06:26.880 --> 00:06:30.060
first big-budget tentpole movie is supremely
entertaining.

00:06:30.560 --> 00:06:34.920
Thanks to an incredible script - and a particularly
brilliant lead performance from Oscar winner

00:06:34.920 --> 00:06:37.500
Brie Larson, the film is an absolute triumph.

00:06:37.980 --> 00:06:42.240
Critics commended Larson for bringing a strong
sense of fun and swagger to the role of Carol

00:06:42.250 --> 00:06:47.440
Danvers, and many reviewers praised the onscreen
chemistry between Larson and Samuel L. Jackson,

00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:50.000
who portrays a digitally de-aged Nick Fury.

00:06:50.900 --> 00:06:52.040
"How long you plan to be in town?"

00:06:52.040 --> 00:06:55.520
"Oh, I'll be out of your hair as soon as I
track down the Skrulls that are infiltrating

00:06:55.520 --> 00:06:56.120
your planet."

00:06:57.760 --> 00:06:58.280
"Skrulls?"

00:06:58.980 --> 00:07:03.200
Reviewers also loved Ben Mendelsohn's performance
as the Skrull warrior Talos.

00:07:03.520 --> 00:07:06.280
The Maine Edge absolutely loved the film,
noting how

00:07:06.600 --> 00:07:10.380
"Captain Marvel could have floundered under
the storytelling load it was asked to shoulder,

00:07:10.380 --> 00:07:15.139
but instead manages to (mostly) soar, giving
us a fun and engaging narrative, some decent

00:07:15.140 --> 00:07:19.640
gags and some solid action set pieces (along
with a killer ‘90s soundtrack)."

00:07:20.740 --> 00:07:24.580
Shazam! just might be the warmest, friendliest,
goofiest superhero film ever.

00:07:24.840 --> 00:07:29.740
Zachary Levi completely sells the notion of
a 14-year-old inhabiting a superhero's body,

00:07:29.740 --> 00:07:31.560
bringing a wide-eyed wonder to the role.

00:07:31.700 --> 00:07:36.580
"Your phone's charged, your phone's charged,
and your phone’s charged…well you think

00:07:36.580 --> 00:07:37.680
you could do better?"

00:07:37.680 --> 00:07:38.180
"The hell…?"

00:07:38.600 --> 00:07:41.780
Critics were quick to embrace the film, with
The Wall Street Journal writing that

00:07:41.880 --> 00:07:45.099
"What Shazam! lacks most obviously is gravitas.

00:07:45.100 --> 00:07:45.600
And grimness.

00:07:45.740 --> 00:07:48.860
Also bleakness, darkness and relentless self-seriousness.

00:07:49.280 --> 00:07:50.820
In other words, it's terrific fun."

00:07:51.240 --> 00:07:55.919
Other critics noted that director David F.
Sandberg masterfully juggled the film's humorous,

00:07:55.920 --> 00:07:57.940
emotional, and action-packed sequences.

00:07:58.180 --> 00:08:02.439
And some reviewers were excited about what
this could mean for the future of the DC Extended

00:08:02.439 --> 00:08:04.689
Universe, with Salon boldly proclaiming that:

00:08:04.689 --> 00:08:07.940
"[Shazam! is] a sign that it is at last ready
to compete with Marvel."

00:08:09.040 --> 00:08:13.960
2014's The Lego Movie was a surprise hit,
and definitely one of the best animated films

00:08:13.960 --> 00:08:14.480
of the year.

00:08:14.740 --> 00:08:18.740
In fact, if we're being honest, it's better
than any film based on building-block toys

00:08:18.740 --> 00:08:19.940
has any right to be.

00:08:20.440 --> 00:08:24.400
This year's The Lego Movie 2: The Second Part
saw the return of the first film's stellar

00:08:24.400 --> 00:08:28.920
voice cast, including Chris Pratt, Elizabeth
Banks, Will Ferrell, and Will Arnett.

00:08:29.349 --> 00:08:33.419
It also added talent like Tiffany Haddish
and Jason Momoa, who reprised his role as

00:08:33.420 --> 00:08:36.120
Aquaman from last year's live-action blockbuster.

00:08:36.580 --> 00:08:40.860
Film Inquiry had nothing but good things to
say about Lego Movie 2: The Second Part, writing

00:08:40.860 --> 00:08:41.360
that:

00:08:41.660 --> 00:08:46.100
"It's a rare film; a perfectly crafted sequel
that expands on the world and mythology of

00:08:46.100 --> 00:08:49.840
its predecessor, but stands firmly on its
own little Lego-legs."

00:08:50.700 --> 00:08:55.040
Dreamworks' How to Train Your Dragon franchise
has garnered the kind of critical praise and

00:08:55.040 --> 00:08:57.980
box-office receipts normally reserved for
Pixar's offerings.

00:08:58.200 --> 00:08:59.840
And there's a very good reason for that.

00:09:00.220 --> 00:09:04.240
Each of the films are technically accomplished
and imbued with a depth of storytelling and

00:09:04.240 --> 00:09:07.180
emotion that's highly uncommon in kids' animated
features.

00:09:07.620 --> 00:09:13.140
The 2010 original and 2014's How to Train
Your Dragon 2 raked in over a billion dollars

00:09:13.140 --> 00:09:13.640
worldwide.

00:09:14.180 --> 00:09:18.520
It took five long years for trilogy capper
The Hidden World to finally arrive, and guess

00:09:18.520 --> 00:09:19.020
what?

00:09:19.260 --> 00:09:22.300
The wait was well worth it, because the film
is really good.

00:09:22.560 --> 00:09:23.640
Critics thought so, too.

00:09:27.480 --> 00:09:29.040
Laramie Movie Scope wrote that

00:09:29.400 --> 00:09:33.820
"[It's a] nice story, combined with marvelous
animation, which has only gotten more sophisticated

00:09:33.829 --> 00:09:37.000
and effective over the years, thanks to advances
in technology."

00:09:37.000 --> 00:09:38.990
Meanwhile, The New York Post proclaimed that

00:09:39.200 --> 00:09:41.940
"The movie could easily be called How To End
Your Trilogy."

00:09:43.040 --> 00:09:47.180
Over the years, animation house Laika Studios
has released quite a few films - and they've

00:09:47.180 --> 00:09:48.480
all been highly unique.

00:09:48.700 --> 00:09:55.440
There's the 2009 hit Coraline, 2012's ParaNorman,
and the well-received 2016 fantasy film Kubo

00:09:55.440 --> 00:09:56.540
and the Two Strings.

00:09:56.680 --> 00:10:01.260
For this year's Missing Link, the studio recruited
ParaNorman director Chris Butler and a talented

00:10:01.260 --> 00:10:05.600
voice cast featuring Hugh Jackman, Zack Galifianakis,
and Zoe Saldana.

00:10:05.920 --> 00:10:09.880
It's the story of a Bigfoot-like creature
who enlists famous explorers to lead him on

00:10:09.880 --> 00:10:11.050
a quest to find his relatives.

00:10:11.520 --> 00:10:12.380
"Can you take me there?"

00:10:12.540 --> 00:10:14.160
"I give you my word."

00:10:14.300 --> 00:10:18.600
The action-packed narrative and sweet humor
really won over reviewers... but the eye-popping

00:10:18.600 --> 00:10:22.340
stop-motion animation is undoubtedly Missing
Link's ace in the hole.

00:10:22.600 --> 00:10:23.880
The website Rave claimed

00:10:24.260 --> 00:10:28.560
"Missing Link continues to prove not only
Laika's visual mastery, but also the studio's

00:10:28.560 --> 00:10:31.480
commitment to telling emotionally resonant
stories."

00:10:31.880 --> 00:10:34.480
Consequence of Sound was equally generous
in its praise:

00:10:34.940 --> 00:10:38.600
"As with so many Laika films, you'll come
for the breathtaking animation, and you'll

00:10:38.600 --> 00:10:42.500
leave both enchanted and surprised by the
big, beating heart beneath it."

00:10:42.720 --> 00:10:46.520
Even the less effusive reviews couldn't help
but gush over the film's visual style.

00:10:46.900 --> 00:10:50.580
Although The Washington Post claimed the film
was "missing something," it also assured readers

00:10:50.580 --> 00:10:51.080
that

00:10:51.440 --> 00:10:55.980
"[The movie] is visually stunning, with well-realized
characters and humor that really does work."

00:10:56.300 --> 00:11:00.640
Sadly, the film's lackluster box office performance
might not be enough to keep the financially

00:11:00.640 --> 00:11:02.360
troubled animation house afloat.

00:11:02.560 --> 00:11:06.920
But as you can see, the film is absolutely
beautiful to look at, and we strongly recommend

00:11:06.920 --> 00:11:08.780
watching the film if you haven't already.

00:11:11.040 --> 00:11:12.380
"A rope would be better."

00:11:12.740 --> 00:11:13.380
"I don't know.

00:11:13.740 --> 00:11:15.580
Three more of them and we could build a ramp."

00:11:16.200 --> 00:11:21.120
The Toy Story series offered up three near-perfect
animated films in the space of fifteen years,

00:11:21.130 --> 00:11:25.680
and after a flawless capper in Toy Story 3
and a nine-year gap, fans could be forgiven

00:11:25.680 --> 00:11:27.860
for being a bit wary of Toy Story 4.

00:11:29.080 --> 00:11:30.820
"So long, partner."

00:11:31.080 --> 00:11:35.420
But Pixar did it once again, fielding a fourth
entry in the saga of Woody, Buzz, and the

00:11:35.420 --> 00:11:40.100
gang that's every bit as heartfelt, lovingly
animated, and hilarious as its predecessors.

00:11:40.420 --> 00:11:42.580
Matthew Norman of the London Evening Standard
wrote,

00:11:43.080 --> 00:11:45.020
"Toy Story 4 is so good it's criminal.

00:11:45.280 --> 00:11:49.500
The legislation it flouts is the law of diminishing
returns which governs movies with numbers

00:11:49.500 --> 00:11:50.580
after their names."

00:11:50.740 --> 00:11:55.200
The film's accolades were many, and covered
every aspect of its production, from the animation

00:11:55.200 --> 00:11:57.740
to the story to its treatment of its new characters.

00:11:58.180 --> 00:12:00.100
NBC News' Ani Bundel wrote,

00:12:00.380 --> 00:12:04.279
"Everyone gets time to tell their story, whether
it's Duke Caboom or Buzz Lightyear."

00:12:04.660 --> 00:12:08.200
But perhaps the highest praise, delivered
by Reno News &amp; Review's Bob Grimm:

00:12:08.520 --> 00:12:09.420
"Is it the last one?

00:12:09.640 --> 00:12:10.200
Oh, come on.

00:12:10.200 --> 00:12:11.800
Just keep making these movies forever."

00:12:12.900 --> 00:12:18.060
2019 has been something special for Marvel
Studios, with three critically acclaimed,

00:12:18.060 --> 00:12:23.240
billion-dollar-plus grossing hits - an unprecedented
feat to bring a close to the three-phase Infinity

00:12:23.240 --> 00:12:23.740
Saga.

00:12:24.060 --> 00:12:28.880
The saga concluded with the sweet, action-packed
coda Spider-Man: Far from Home, the first

00:12:28.880 --> 00:12:32.700
live-action Spidey vehicle to ever break a
billion at the global box office.

00:12:32.960 --> 00:12:36.760
Critics were taken with how the film played
with the conventions of its genre, continually

00:12:36.760 --> 00:12:41.800
upending audience expectations - especially
with respect to its alleged secondary "hero,"

00:12:41.970 --> 00:12:47.260
the devious Mysterio played by Jake Gyllenhaal,
whose performance garnered widespread praise,

00:12:47.260 --> 00:12:48.540
as did star Tom Holland.

00:12:48.940 --> 00:12:53.290
The Spider-Man series remains a favorite among
Marvel fans, thanks to director Jon Watts'

00:12:53.290 --> 00:12:55.220
distinctive John Hughes-like tone.

00:12:55.630 --> 00:13:00.500
For all its butt-kicking spectacle, many critics
praised it as simply a delightful coming-of-age

00:13:00.519 --> 00:13:04.730
comedy which just happened to also feature
some of the most jaw-dropping post-credits

00:13:04.730 --> 00:13:06.640
scenes the MCU has yet offered.

00:13:07.600 --> 00:13:12.260
Producer Sam Raimi and director Alexandre
Aja have a highly admirable horror pedigree,

00:13:12.260 --> 00:13:17.040
and Crawl sported a unique premise: trapped
in her father's home during a massive hurricane

00:13:17.040 --> 00:13:21.540
with flood waters on the rise, a woman gets
some unexpected company in the form of some

00:13:21.540 --> 00:13:22.760
hungry alligators.

00:13:22.930 --> 00:13:26.760
The flick milks this premise for all it's
worth, and critics generally agreed that the

00:13:26.760 --> 00:13:30.260
result was a white-knuckle fright-fest rich
with tension and chills.

00:13:30.480 --> 00:13:35.240
Lead Kaya Scoledario was praised for her energetic
performance, helping to whisk the film along

00:13:35.240 --> 00:13:37.420
from one terrifying set piece to the next.

00:13:37.690 --> 00:13:41.420
As many of the flick's champions noted, it's
tough to go wrong in the hands of skilled

00:13:41.420 --> 00:13:44.140
filmmakers with all of these ingredients at
their disposal.

00:13:44.420 --> 00:13:46.480
The Movie Blog's Emmanuel Noisette wrote:

00:13:46.760 --> 00:13:51.000
"When you combine a natural disaster, scary
predators, [and] confined spaces, all through

00:13:51.000 --> 00:13:54.320
a personal human lens, you have a solid film
on your hands."

00:13:54.440 --> 00:13:58.440
While the movie had its requisite share of
detractors, its defenders noted that Crawl

00:13:58.440 --> 00:14:00.300
delivered exactly what it promised.

00:14:00.600 --> 00:14:02.620
Pete Vonder Haar of Houston Press wrote:

00:14:02.860 --> 00:14:07.279
"If you go into Crawl hoping to see a woman
improbably outswim large, pissed-off reptiles,

00:14:07.279 --> 00:14:12.050
or watch ATM thieves get messily devoured,
you're in for one of the most enjoyable theatrical

00:14:12.050 --> 00:14:13.520
experiences of the year."

